        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Exposure to microgravity results in multiple physiological and anatomical changes, some of which alter brain and ocular physiology and structure. More than half of astronauts exposed to long-duration spaceflight present with symptoms of the visual impairment and intracranial pressure (VIIP) syndrome, but the etiology and long-term implications of these changes are unknown. Identifying the pathophysiological mechanisms and developing an effective countermeasure for the spaceflight-induced adverse physiological effects related to fluid redistribution and VIIP syndrome is a NASA priority due to the high-risk potential on mission success.<p>

This experiment will investigate the effects of a 60-day exposure to simulated microgravity using 6° head down tilt bedrest (HDBR) on the cerebral, ocular and vestibular systems with and without the intervention of intermittent artificial gravity (AG). Our overarching objective is to reveal the effects of HDBR-induced headward fluid shifts as well as the reversal of these hydrostatic pressure gradients with AG using innovative, integrated techniques to provide novel insights into the physiological adaptations during simulated microgravity.</p><p>

This experiment aims to:</p><p>

1. determine the between-group effects of 60 days HDBR alone versus HDBR with AG (2
different regimens) on the cerebral, ocular, and sensorimotor systems<br>

2. determine the within-group effects of the experimental conditions (60 days HDBR +/-
AG) on the cerebral, ocular and sensorimotor systems compared to pre HDBR baseline<br>

3. Assess the within and between group transient effects of AG (intervention groups) on
cerebral and ocular systems using non-invasive technologies in the peri-centrifuge period</p><p>

APPROACH:<br>
This experiment will be a randomized interventional, observational study with 3 groups made up of 8 subjects each.</p><p>

Group 1: 6° HDBR with no centrifugation (control)<br>
Group 2: 6° HDBR with supine centrifugation at +1Gz at the heart for 30 minutes per day (intervention 1)<br>
Group 3: 6° HDBR with supine centrifugation at +1Gz at the heart for 6 bouts of 5 minutes (separated by 5 minutes of rest) per day (intervention 2)</p><p>

Subjects will have a 2 week pre-HDBR baseline period for assessment of nutritional, neurological, ocular, cardiopulmonary, vestibular, and other systemic organ system function as per the NASA standard measures. This test period will be followed by a phase-in period for the HDBR +/- AG conditions of 2 subjects per day x 6 days. Subjects will be assigned to one of the 3 conditions using computerized randomization methods. The proportion of subjects will be 1:1 male to female to avoid gender selection bias. The phase-in methodology is used in other German Aerospace Center (DLR) studies and allows procedures and methods to be conducted at standard times during the day to avoid circadian rhythm and other time-based effects.</p><p>

RESULTS:<br>
Experiment in progress. Results for this study are not yet available.</p>
        </div>

